# Ignore everything by default
/**/*

# The Java 8 Lombok compatibility doclet is source, not a generated artifact.
!workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/java8/
!workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/java8/LegacyExtractor.java
!workspace/compiler/languages/tests/go/fixtures/
!workspace/compiler/languages/tests/go/fixtures/cross-target/
!workspace/compiler/languages/tests/go/fixtures/cross-target/*

# Allow all directories for traversal
!**/

# Deny common build/cache directories
**/.DS_Store
**/target/
**/.cache/
**/.git/
# Buck2 persistent on-disk sqlite dir-cache (buckconfig.d/cache.buckconfig dir = .buck-cache/sqlite).
# Lives outside buck-out intentionally to survive `buck2 clean`; must be gitignored.
**/.buck-cache/

# Deny Nix symlinks and generated files
**/result
**/result-*

# Deny temporary/log files
**/*.log

# Root level files
!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/LICENSE
!/justfile
!/default.nix
!/here
!/.envrc
!/.editorconfig

# Rust
!/**/*.rs
!/**/rustfmt.toml
# Workspace + crate manifests required for cargo / rustService builds.
# (Flake source filtering follows git — without these, `nix build .#server`
# has Cargo.lock but no Cargo.toml and cargo fails immediately.)
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/workspace/**/Cargo.toml
!/workspace/gui/Cargo.lock
# Crates under /crates need the same allowance — without it a new crate's
# manifest is silently ignored and the workspace fails to load.
!/crates/**/Cargo.toml
# workspace/gui is a standalone Cargo workspace with its own lockfile — that is
# the whole point of the split, since it is what keeps gpui out of the root
# workspace's graph. The root allowance above is anchored at `/Cargo.lock` and
# does not reach it, so the lockfile pinning gpui, gpui-component and the whole
# wgpu stack was untracked: every fresh checkout re-resolved them, which is not
# a lockfile at all. It is an application, so the lockfile belongs in the tree.
!/workspace/gui/Cargo.lock
# Manifests that tests scaffold at runtime stay ignored.
/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/Cargo.toml

# C/C++ parser fixtures
!/compiler/src/core/clang/tests/**/*.c
!/compiler/src/core/clang/tests/**/*.cpp
!/compiler/src/core/clang/tests/**/compile_commands.json

# Vendored C sources that are the ONLY definition of symbols the Rust tree
# declares as `extern "C"`. These were absent from the checkout, and because
# both qdrant-edge build scripts warned-and-skipped instead of failing, the
# gap surfaced ~200 lines later as six undefined symbols at `driver`'s link
# step (LIMITATIONS L7). `build_common.rs::require_vendored_source` now makes
# that a hard build error, but the error only helps if the sources can be
# committed in the first place — the blanket `/**/*` rule above silently
# swallowed them, so `git add` no-opped and every clean checkout rebuilt the
# same breakage. ~37 KB total.
!/workspace/vendor/**/cpp/**/*.c
!/workspace/vendor/**/cpp/**/*.h

# The Vaporetto tokenizer model `japanese.rs` pulls in via `include_bytes!`.
# What was on disk was a 4-byte file containing the ASCII text `STUB`, which
# compiles cleanly and then panics inside `Model::read_slice(MODEL).unwrap()`
# on the first Japanese tokenization. The real artifact is 817,859 bytes and
# is verified against `MODEL_CHECKSUM`, declared in `japanese.rs` itself, so
# a wrong or truncated copy fails loudly rather than at first use. Binary and
# large for a source tree, tracked deliberately: the alternative is an
# unpinned network fetch in a build script, or the stub coming back.
!/workspace/vendor/**/tokenizer/*.model

# DoltLite vendoring: the pin and the fetcher, NOT the amalgamation.
#
# `manifest.toml` pins upstream v0.11.41 (commit e2be0af1…) plus the archive
# and per-file sha256s; `fetch.nu` fetches, verifies **before** extracting, and
# installs — the same verify-or-abort discipline as `nix build .#checks.corpus`, so no
# build script ever performs an unpinned network fetch. Without these two in
# the tree the pin is unreproducible and `build.rs` finds no amalgamation, at
# which point `sys.rs` emits no C externs and every `Connection::open` returns
# `EngineError::EngineNotLinked` — i.e. the whole guard degrades to "no engine"
# rather than to the silent wrong-engine bug it exists to prevent.
#
# Deliberately NOT allow-listed, and this is a policy choice rather than an
# oversight: `doltlite.c` (11.7 MB), `doltlite.h` (695 KB), `doltliteext.h`
# (39 KB). All three are *generated*, hash-pinned by the manifest above, and
# restored by `nu workspace/vendor/doltlite/fetch.nu` in one command. Contrast
# the Vaporetto tokenizer model below, which IS committed: that artifact has no
# upstream fetcher and its absence produced a 4-byte `STUB` that compiled fine
# and panicked at first use. Here the absence is typed and loud, so the 11.7 MB
# buys nothing.
!/workspace/vendor/doltlite/manifest.toml
!/workspace/vendor/doltlite/fetch.nu

# Nix
!/flake.nix
!/flake.lock
# Shared Nix helpers and Buck/Nix bridge.
!/nix/
!/nix/**
# Workspace package recipes + images.
!/workspace/**/*.nix
# Integration checks (Nix glue + Nushell scripts/modules).
!/tests/**/*.nix
!/tests/**/*.nu
# lindsey's bundled themes. A theme is *data* — `workspace/gui/src/theme/spec.rs`
# deserialises these and `registry.rs` `include_str!`s them into `BUNDLED`, so a
# theme is added by dropping a file here, with no Rust change.
#
# That makes them source, and this deny-all list would otherwise swallow them:
# `include_str!` reads the working tree, so the build stays green locally while
# `git add -A` silently skips the files and the tree fails to compile for anyone
# else. Exactly the packaging defect class that produced "HEAD did not compile"
# on 2026-08-08 — a file present locally, absent from the index, and no signal
# until someone clones.
!/workspace/gui/assets/
!/workspace/gui/assets/themes/*.json

# Vendored gpui-component: path-dep of lindsey. Icons are `RustEmbed`'d and
# advertised to dependents at build time; highlighter queries and theme JSON
# are `include_str!`'d. The deny-all would leave a green local GUI build and
# a `nix build .#lindsey-app` that panics in `gpui-component-assets`'s
# build.rs.
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/**/*.svg
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/**/*.json
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/**/*.scm
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/**/*.md
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component-fork.patch

# The brand mark. Every visual artefact lindsey ships with — app icon, DMG
# volume icon, DMG background art — is generated FROM this one file (see
# workspace/gui/packaging/), so it is source, not a build output. It was
# caught by the deny-all above with no allow-list entry of its own: `git
# check-ignore -v workspace/gui/assets/logo.svg` reported it ignored, and `git status` could not
# see it, which is the exact same trap the theme JSONs hit above — a file
# present locally, invisible to the index, gone on a fresh clone.
!/workspace/gui/assets/logo.svg

# lindsey's macOS packaging: cargo-bundle's `osx_dmg_background` key
# (workspace/gui/Cargo.toml) reads dmg-background.svg directly off disk at
# `cargo bundle` time, and generate_dmg_background.py is what derives it from
# /workspace/gui/assets/logo.svg. Both are source for the same reason the themes above are: no
# Rust reads them, so nothing forces them to be tracked except this rule, and
# without it a fresh clone builds a DMG with no background and no one is told
# why. The generated .icns/.tiff/.dmg themselves are NOT allow-listed — those
# are build output under workspace/gui/target/bundle/, already denied by
# `**/target/` above.
!/workspace/gui/packaging/
!/workspace/gui/packaging/*.svg
!/workspace/gui/packaging/*.py

# The vendored gpui-component fork, in full.
#
# `workspace/vendor/README.md` lists this package as "Sources Committed: Yes".
# It was not. The deny-all above keeps `.rs` (allowed globally) and drops
# everything else, so the fork reached the tree missing 121 files: 99 icon SVGs,
# 17 tree-sitter `.scm` query files, the default theme and colour JSON, the
# locale catalogue, and its LICENSE-APACHE. The GUI could not build from a clean
# clone on any platform —
#
#   expected default icons at …/crates/assets/assets/icons, but the directory
#   is missing
#   couldn't read …/src/theme/./default-theme.json
#
# Allowed as a directory rather than by extension, because "which extensions
# does a vendored third-party crate need?" is not a question this file can keep
# answering correctly — it already got it wrong for four of them. Build output
# is re-denied below, since a later rule wins.
!/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/crates/**
/workspace/vendor/gpui-component/**/target/

# lindsey's Linux packaging: the desktop entry, the AppStream metainfo, the
# installer and the run wrapper. Allowed as a *directory* rather than by
# extension, which the rules above do and this one deliberately does not.
#
# Extension allow-lists have now failed three times in this one directory: the
# run wrapper has no extension at all, and `install.sh` and the `.metainfo.xml`
# were both silently skipped by `git add` — the second time producing a commit
# whose message described two files that were not in it. Everything under this
# path is hand-written source with no build output in it, so the honest rule is
# the directory.
!/workspace/gui/packaging/linux/**

# Data and Configuration Artifacts
# TODO: Remove from pipeline (currently used for debugging/regression tracking)
!/**/example.json
!/**/out.json
!/**/docs.json
!/**/schema.json

# Documentation
!/**/*.md

# Trustfall schema and query sources
!/**/*.graphql
!/**/*.trustfall

# Freedesktop entries shipped with the GUI (workspace/gui/packaging/linux). The
# window's `app_id` is matched against the installed entry by name, so an
# untracked one means the app loses its icon on every fresh checkout.
!/**/*.desktop

# The Linux run wrapper, which puts the host's GPU driver on the loader's path
# for the GUI process only. Listed by name because it has no extension, and
# every allow-rule in this file keys off one — `git add -A` silently skipped it
# on the first attempt, which is the same trap the themes and the query-plane
# sources hit, arriving this time on a file added to fix a packaging problem.
!/workspace/gui/packaging/linux/run-lindsey

# Go / Java oracle producer sources. Unlike the Rust producer (compiled from
# committed .rs), the Go and Java oracles ship as source compiled by Buck2's
# go_binary / java_library. They must be tracked or fresh checkouts / git
# worktrees build an empty target ("not package main" / "expected exactly one
# default output").
# NOTE: `workspace/compiler/compile/` no longer exists (folded into
# `workspace/compiler/languages/` — see git history at 23530d7). These two
# patterns are dead but harmless; kept so a future revert of that migration
# does not silently lose tracking. The Go oracle's new home is covered below.
!/workspace/compiler/compile/**/*.go
!/workspace/compiler/compile/**/*.java
!/workspace/compiler/compile/**/go.mod
!/workspace/compiler/compile/**/go.sum
# Go oracle source at its post-migration home. The compiled `nudox-go-oracle`
# binary this produces is NOT allow-listed here — it stays denied by the
# default-deny rule at the top of this file, same as every other build
# artifact.
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/go/*.go
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/go/go.mod
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/go/go.sum
# Java oracle source at its post-migration home (JAVELIN restoration from git
# history at 23530d7^) — same reasoning as the Go oracle above: `javac`
# (via build.rs) compiles these two committed .java files at crate build
# time; the resulting `.class` files land under Cargo's own `OUT_DIR` and
# stay denied by the default-deny rule, same as every other build artifact.
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/java/*.java

# C# Roslyn oracle — unlike Go and Java above, this was NOT recovered from
# history (only a BUCK file ever existed); it is new source and is the sole copy.
# Single-level `*` is deliberate: `**/` would also un-ignore `obj/**/*.g.cs` and
# the generated `AssemblyInfo.cs`, which must stay denied as build artifacts.
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/csharp/*.cs
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/csharp/oracle.csproj
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/csharp/packages.lock.json
!/workspace/compiler/languages/oracle/csharp/deps.json
!/workspace/compiler/languages/tests/csharp/fixture/*.cs
!/workspace/compiler/languages/tests/csharp/fixture/fixture.csproj

# Producer test fixtures: source files for the TypeScript / Nix producers are
# committed (mirroring the committed Rust `.rs` fixtures). Manifests that tests
# scaffold at runtime (rust `Cargo.toml`) stay ignored; these are the inputs the
# producers read directly, so they must live in the tree.
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.ts
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.tsx
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.mts
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.cts
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.d.ts
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/package.json
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.nix
# Go / Java producer test fixtures (mirror the .ts / .nix fixtures above).
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.go
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/*.java
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/go.mod
!/workspace/compiler/tests/fixtures/**/go.sum
# nudox-languages's Java producer's own real-library fixture (Gson 2.11.0,
# vendored — see workspace/compiler/languages/tests/java/producer_tests.rs's
# module doc for exactly what is/isn't real Gson source) plus small
# hand-written-but-really-compiled supplementary fixtures (records/sealed/
# module-info; a JEP 467 probe). Committed, not `workspace/compiler/tests/
# fixtures/` above, because these are specific to this one producer. Committed
# at all — rather than fetched over the network at test time — so a fresh
# checkout can run `cargo test -p nudox-languages` hermetically.
!/workspace/compiler/languages/tests/java/fixtures/**/*.java
!/workspace/compiler/languages/tests/java/fixtures/**/LICENSE-gson.txt
# Index ingest test fixtures (moved from workspace/ingestor).
!/workspace/index/tests/fixtures/*.json

# The curated `cpp` alias seed. This is NOT a fixture — `ecosystem/cpp/alias.rs`
# pulls it in with `include_str!`, so it is a build input: without it the whole
# `index` crate fails to compile. The blanket `/**/*` rule at the top would
# swallow it, `git add` would silently no-op, and a fresh clone would be back to
# the "couldn't read cpp_alias_seed.ron" error that kept this crate from
# building at all.
!/workspace/index/ecosystem/assets/*.ron

# insta snapshot expectations (accepted `.snap` files) for the producer /
# renderer snapshot tests. These are the committed test baselines; insta's
# transient `.snap.new` pending files end in `.new` so this glob leaves them
# ignored.
!/**/*.snap

# Patch files for Buck2 genrules
!/**/*.patch

# Allow empty .keep files
!/**/.keep
!/.config/scripts/*.nu
!/.config/scripts/nix-offline-check.sh
!/.forgejo/
!/.forgejo/workflows/
!/.forgejo/workflows/*.yml
# The stub embeddings server local-backends.nu spawns for the `index`
# server-integration "Live" tier (docs/TESTING.md) -- source, not a build
# output; without this carve-out the blanket `/**/*` rule swallows it the
# same way it would swallow the .nu scripts above.
!/.config/scripts/*.py
# cargo-nextest configuration. Without this the blanket `/**/*` rule at the top
# swallows it, `git add .config/nextest.toml` silently no-ops, and a fresh clone
# gets no test groups at all — which is how the GUI and real-crate serialisation
# would quietly stop being enforced.
!/.config/nextest.toml
!/.config/nextest-gui.toml
!/.vscode/settings.json

